name: reading files
boss: CatMan
flavor: Everything you wrote, I read back.
brief: cat dumps a whole file. head and tail take the ends of it. tac reverses
brief: it. less lets you move around inside one too big to dump. strings pulls
brief: the readable text out of a file that is not text at all.
brief:
brief: The habit worth building: reach for head before cat. A file you did not
brief: write can be a gigabyte, and cat will happily send all of it.
---
q: {print|show|dump} the contents of @txt@
a: cat @txt@
out: rotate the logs
ref: cat
teach: cat
---
q: show @txt@ with every line numbered
a: cat -n @txt@
out: ^ *1
ref: cat
teach: cat -n
---
q: show @txt@ numbering only the lines that are not blank
a: cat -b @txt@
out: ^ *1
ref: cat
teach: cat -b
---
q: show @cfg@ with a $ marking the end of every line
a: cat -e @cfg@
out: \$$
ref: cat
teach: cat -e
hint: cat calls this "End each line with $"
---
q: show the first ten lines of @log@
a: head @log@
out: service started
ref: head
teach: head
hint: ten is what head does with no arguments
---
q: show the first @n@ lines of @log@
a: head -n @n@ @log@
a: head -@n@ @log@
out: service started
ref: head
teach: head -n
---
q: show the first 20 bytes of @log@
a: head -c 20 @log@
out: 2024-03-01
ref: head
teach: head -c
hint: c for characters
---
q: show the last ten lines of @log@
a: tail @log@
out: out of memory
ref: tail
teach: tail
---
q: show the last @n3@ lines of @log@
a: tail -n @n3@ @log@
a: tail -@n3@ @log@
out: .
ref: tail
teach: tail -n
---
q: show @log@ from line 5 onward -- not the last 5, the fifth to the end
a: tail -n +5 @log@
out: connection refused
ref: tail
teach: tail -n
hint: a plus sign in front of the number changes it from "last N" to "from N"
---
q: watch @log@ and keep printing new lines as they are appended
a: tail -f @log@
ref: tail
teach: tail -f
hint: f for follow. this one never exits -- Ctrl+c is how you leave.
---
q: print @txt@ with its lines in reverse order, last line first
a: tac @txt@
out: verify the root hash
ref: tac
teach: tac
hint: it is cat backwards, which is also what it does
---
q: pull the readable text out of @bin@, which is not a text file
a: strings @bin@
out: unlocked-by-strings
ref: strings
teach: strings
---
q: show only strings in @bin@ that are at least 5 characters long
a: strings -n 5 @bin@
out: unlocked-by-strings
ref: strings
teach: strings -n
---
q: show the strings in @bin@ with the byte offset each one was found at
a: strings -t d @bin@
a: strings -t x @bin@
out: unlocked-by-strings
ref: strings
teach: strings -t
hint: t takes a radix -- d decimal, x hex, o octal
---
q: open @log@ in the pager
a: less @log@
ref: less
teach: less
hint: q leaves, /pattern searches, g and G jump to the ends
---
q: open @log@ in the pager with line numbers shown
a: less -N @log@
ref: less
teach: less -N
---
q: open @log@ in the pager without wrapping long lines
a: less -S @log@
ref: less
teach: less -S
---
q: open @log@ in the pager, searching case-insensitively
a: less -I @log@
ref: less
teach: less -I
---
q: show the last 20 bytes of @log@ rather than the last lines
a: tail -c 20 @log@
out: .
ref: tail
teach: tail -c
---
q: follow @log@ by name, so it keeps working if the file is rotated and replaced
a: tail -F @log@
ref: tail
teach: tail -F
hint: capital F reopens the name; lowercase f holds the old file open forever
---
q: show the byte offset of every string found in @bin@
a: strings -o @bin@
out: [0-9]
ref: strings
teach: strings -o
